Reading a unit's magnitude, and interpolating what comes back.

A unit's members are now looked up on the declaration the way a struct's are:
`ir.UnitDef.Fields` is one float per base, so `m.px` is a float. It had no
member table at all before, so every select on a unit -- the valid `.px`
exactly as much as the invented `.value` -- typed `dyn`, and `dyn` is what
`interpPartAlreadyString` answers yes to. That is the whole of this bug: the
string conversion every other numeric operand gets was skipped for the one
spelling a unit's magnitude has.

The `px is` line below is the fix and the `w is` line beside it is the control.
Before, Go emitted `("px is " + m.m.Px)` -- a string plus a float64, which does
not compile -- while the plain float on the next line got its `fmt.Sprint`.
Kotlin concatenated the raw `Double`, legal and so silently formatted unlike
every other float on the page, and JavaScript the raw number. Typing the select
fixed all three at once; no backend needed a case, which is the evidence that
the defect was the missing member table and not three emitters.

`sum` is the multi-base half, and the reason the member reads are not all off
literals. A value arithmetic built carries a magnitude in px and another in em,
so `sum.px` and `sum.em` are two different numbers off one record and each
target has to add base by base rather than reduce to a single total. The
interpreter was the backend that reduced, which is what
`cmd/sngl/testdata/unit_magnitude_compiles.txt` runs this same shape to say.

`float(t)` and `int(d)` are the single-base half. A single-base unit is a plain
number on every target and carries no field at all, so a conversion is how its
magnitude is read, and a reader reaching for `t.tk` should be able to see that
here. What `int(d)` *emits* is a separate subject with its own golden
(`unit_duration_cast.txtar`): a duration is a `time.Duration` in Go, so the
cast divides the nanoseconds out.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `"px is " \+ m\.` -- a float64 concatenated onto a string is not Go; the per-base read has to arrive through fmt.Sprint like any other number
deny out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt `"px is " \+ m__inst0\.px` -- Kotlin would compile this and format it unlike every other float on the page
deny out/none/html/index.html `"px is " \+ state\.m__inst0\.px` -- JS would render the raw number rather than the String() every other numeric interpolation gets

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:time"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

unit tick {
    tk
}

component main ui.node {
    var m ui.measurement = 3px
    var r ui.measurement = 2rem
    var sum ui.measurement = m + r
    var t tick = 4tk
    var d time.duration = 500ms
    var w = 2.5

    ui.text(value="px is {m.px}")
    ui.text(value="w is {w}")
    ui.text(value="em is {m.em}")
    ui.text(value="sum px is {sum.px}")
    ui.text(value="sum em is {sum.em}")
    ui.text(value="tk is {float(t)}")
    ui.text(value="ms is {int(d)}")
}

ui.window {
    main
}
